# Never touch production servers

<!-- core -->
Never access, run commands on, or perform actions against any PRODUCTION server or system, in any
project — no SSH, no remote exec, no prod DB client, no prod cloud console or CLI call, no route at
all. If a task needs a production action, hand back the exact command for the user to run. If their
run errors, wait for the pasted output before deciding anything — never guess or retry blind. If you
cannot tell whether a target is production, treat it as production and ask. One route stays open:
a public, read-only HTTP request to a production URL — a `GET` or `HEAD` with no credentials, the
request any browser makes — is looking, not acting, and is run directly rather than handed back.

<!-- /core -->

## What is closed

Every route, not a list of blocked tools:

- SSH or any remote shell into a production host.
- Remote exec — `kubectl exec`, `docker exec`, a CI job you trigger, a runner, a debug endpoint.
- Production database clients, psql/mysql sessions, migration runners, ORM consoles.
- Cloud console or CLI calls against production projects (`gcloud`, `aws`, `az`, `flyctl`, …),
  including read-only ones.
- Anything else that reaches production, however indirect. The rule is about the target, not the
  verb — with the one exception below.

## What stays open

A public, read-only HTTP request to a production URL: `curl` with `GET` or `HEAD`, no token, no
cookie, no credentials of any kind, against a URL anyone on the internet can load — a page, a static
asset, a response header, a status code. It changes nothing and reveals nothing a browser would not,
so run it yourself; handing it back costs the user a turn per fact. Anything carrying credentials,
any other method, or any non-public endpoint stays closed.

## What to do instead

Hand back the exact steps for a human to run: the literal command, what to type at each prompt, what
a correct result looks like. An instruction the user has to translate before running is not done
work.

## When their run errors

Stop and wait for the pasted output. Do not guess at the cause, do not offer a retry to run blind,
do not suggest a variant command in the same breath. Read the actual error first, then decide.

## Uncertain whether a target is production

Treat it as production and ask. The cost of asking is one message; the cost of guessing wrong is
unbounded and often irreversible.

Applies globally, all projects, no exceptions.
